using SteambirdsLauncher.UI;

namespace SteambirdsLauncher.EnemyAi;

// The help of the Enemy AI tab (Help menu, F1). English texts: the window translates every paragraph with L.T, so each
// paragraph is a line of lang\<code>.json. A paragraph starting with "## " is a sub-heading and "- " an item of a list.
internal static class AiHelp
{
    public static readonly IReadOnlyList<HelpTopic> Topics = new[]
    {
        new HelpTopic("What the Enemy AI editor does",
            "An enemy of the game is a unit (its art, health, speed and guns) plus an AI: the rules that say where it flies and when it shoots. The original AI only ran on the original servers, so here the AI is rebuilt from files that anyone can make, test and share.",
            "This tab makes those files and tests them without opening the game: the simulator on the right flies the real unit with the real guns against a plane that you control, using the same code the server runs.",
            "## How the pieces fit",
            "- A behavior is one way of acting: how to move, whether to shoot and with which guns, and whether to blow up when the player is close.",
            "- An AI is a set of states (for example wait, chase, retreat). Each state acts like a behavior, and has transitions that say when to change to another state.",
            "- A unit type of a map (or of enemies.json) points to an AI with its \"ai\" field. The server gives that AI to every enemy of that type.",
            "## The three areas",
            "- Left: the list of files (AIs and behaviors) and what to do with them.",
            "- Middle: the editor of the AI or behavior that runs on the unit, and below it the list of problems of the file.",
            "- Right: the simulator, to see what you built act.",
            "## Where the files are",
            "Every AI is a .json file in server\\ai\\brains and every behavior is one in server\\ai\\behaviors. The file name is the id. The running server reads the folder again by itself when a file changes: enemies that are born afterwards use the new version. Enemies that are already alive keep the old one."),

        new HelpTopic("The unit bar",
            "The Unit box chooses what the simulator flies, and also what the editor is allowed to edit: the editor always shows the AI of the unit in the simulator.",
            "## What is in the list",
            "- \"type:\" entries are the enemy types of enemies.json, with the game unit, the speed / turn / fire rate values, the aim lead and the AI the server really gives them.",
            "- \"map type:\" entries are the unit types of a map, put there by the button \"Load unit in the AI editor\" of the map editor. It is the way to test an AI on the exact enemy of the map you are making.",
            "- The other entries are plain game units. They have no AI of their own, so they are good for trying any AI on any plane.",
            "## Armed units only",
            "Hides the units that have no guns. Turn it off to try an AI on a unit that only flies.",
            "## What the line under the box says",
            "It tells which file runs on the unit: its own AI (green), a file you loaded just to test it (yellow), or a problem such as an AI id with no file (red). Anything you edit is the file shown there.",
            "## Back to the unit's AI",
            "Appears while a file is loaded on the unit. It stops the test and shows the unit's own AI again. If the file has changes that are not saved it asks what to do first."),

        new HelpTopic("The list of files",
            "The tree on the left has the AIs and the behaviors that exist in the ai folder. A file with an error is written in red: it cannot be edited here until the .json is fixed with a text editor and Reload is used.",
            "## Load on the unit (or double-click)",
            "Puts the selected AI or behavior on the unit in the simulator, to edit it and test it there. It does not change which AI a unit type uses in any map or in enemies.json. A behavior alone runs as an AI with one state.",
            "## Copy id",
            "Copies the id of the selected file. To use it in the game, write that id in the \"ai\" field of a unit type (map editor, Units, Enemy AI) or of enemies.json. For a behavior, pick it in the \"Behavior (file)\" field of a state.",
            "## What changes in the game",
            "Nothing, until the file is saved: the editor works on a copy in memory. After Save, the server (if it is running) reads the new file and the next enemies that are born with that AI use it."),

        new HelpTopic("States",
            "An AI starts in its \"First state\" and stays in a state until one of its transitions says to leave. Only one state is active at a time.",
            "## The States list",
            "- Add makes a new state with an orbit movement; Duplicate copies the selected one; Delete removes it (a transition that pointed to it is left pointing to nothing, and the problems list says so).",
            "- The order of the list does not matter, except that the first state is the one named in \"First state\".",
            "## State tab",
            "- State name: the name transitions and \"First state\" use. Renaming it renames those references.",
            "- Behavior (file): the state acts like that behavior file. Empty means the state has its own behavior (Behavior values tab).",
            "- Blows up when it starts: the unit explodes as soon as it enters the state, like a fuse. In the game the explosion gives no XP and no loot.",
            "- Chat message: text shown in the game chat to the players when the state starts.",
            "- Speech (bark key): key of a speech line of the client, said by the unit when the state starts.",
            "## Behavior values tab",
            "Movement, weapons and contact of the state, the same fields a behavior has. They are only used while the state has no behavior file.",
            "## What it causes in the game",
            "The movement tells the enemy where to fly, the weapons tell which guns may shoot, and a state change is what makes an enemy look alive: for example sleeping until the player is close, attacking, and running away when it is hurt."),

        new HelpTopic("Transitions and conditions",
            "Transitions are checked in order, all the time. The first transition whose conditions are all true changes the state. A transition with no conditions happens at once.",
            "## Go to state",
            "The state the unit changes to when every condition of the transition is true.",
            "## Conditions (all must be true)",
            "- Always: always true.",
            "- Time in this state (s): true after that many seconds in the state. With \"Up to\" filled, the time is picked at random between the two numbers each time the state starts, so enemies do not all act together.",
            "- Player closer than / farther than: distance to the nearest player, in map units (a tile is 12 units).",
            "- Has a player to look at: true while a live player is in the dungeon. With \"Not\" it means the player is gone.",
            "- Health below / above (0-1): the health fraction of the unit (0.5 is half of its health).",
            "- Was hit in the last (s): true when the unit took damage in the last seconds.",
            "- Facing the player (degrees): true when the player is inside that angle in front of the unit.",
            "- Own summons alive at most: true when the units this unit summoned that are still alive number at most that value (0 means all of them are dead).",
            "- Chance per second (0-1): every second the transition happens with that probability.",
            "## Not",
            "Reverses the condition: it is true when the condition is NOT met.",
            "## Example",
            "wait -> attack when the player is closer than 60. attack -> wait when \"has a player\" is not met. In the game the enemy hovers around its home until a player comes near, then fights, and goes back to rest when the player leaves."),

        new HelpTopic("Summons, shield and look",
            "These belong to the state, not to a behavior file, so they have their own tab (Summons and shield). They are what the bosses did with their old \"phases\", now part of the AI.",
            "## Summons",
            "- Summons (unit type): the type the unit calls when the state starts. It can be a type of enemies.json or of the map the unit is in (a dungeon map's own types are tried first).",
            "- Amount each time: 1 to 10 units, placed behind the summoner. They stay in its group and room.",
            "- Again every (s): 0 summons only when the state starts. Otherwise it summons again every that many seconds while the state lasts.",
            "- At most alive: no new summons while that many of its own summons are alive (0 is no limit). The condition \"Own summons alive at most\" lets a transition wait for the summons to die.",
            "## Shield",
            "- Shield: the unit ignores all damage. Shield lasts (s) says for how long; 0 means the whole time it is in the state.",
            "- Shield look: the client effect shown on the unit while the shield is up (empty is tankShield).",
            "## Look and weapons",
            "- Visual effect from here on: a client effect (statefulEffects, for example detectionZone_1) the unit shows from this state on. It stays until another state changes it.",
            "- Homing bullets: on or off from this state on. Empty keeps what the unit has.",
            "The simulator shows the summons as a counter (they are not flown: each one falls after 12 seconds, or press Destroy summons), draws the shield as a ring and shows the effect in its HUD."),

        new HelpTopic("Phases inside an AI",
            "A boss with phases is an AI with a state per phase. There is no special \"phase\" object: a phase is a state that something leads to when the health drops.",
            "## How to make one",
            "- A first state with the normal fight (for a boss that flies with a built-in flight, use the move mode Built-in flight with the builtin field empty).",
            "- A state per phase. In each one, say what the phase does: summons, shield, a chat message or speech line, other weapons or fire rate, homing bullets, a new look.",
            "- A transition from each state to the next: condition Health below (0-1), for example 0.66, 0.33... Phases that are crossed at once (a big hit) all happen, in order.",
            "## Compared with the old phases",
            "The old \"phases\" of a unit type are not read by the server any more; the 24 bosses of the shipped maps were converted into files named <map>_<type> (for example dungeon_ice_iceboss). The Graph view shows the phases as a chain, which is the quickest way to check one.",
            "A phase does not have to be a straight line: a state can go back (the shield ends and it returns to the fight), branch by chance, or wait for its summons to die (Own summons alive at most 0)."),

        new HelpTopic("The graph view",
            "The tab Graph (next to States and transitions) draws the AI as a graph: a box for each state and an arrow for each transition, with its conditions written on it. The list of states is still where everything is edited; the graph is a way to see the whole machine.",
            "## Reading it",
            "- The first state has a yellow border and a mark. Under the name come its movement and, if any, summons, shield and effect.",
            "- Arrows leave the state where the transition is written and point at the state it goes to. Two arrows in opposite directions take opposite sides. An arrow that returns to its own state is drawn as a loop.",
            "## Clicking a state",
            "- It is selected in the list too, so the other tabs show it.",
            "- Its outgoing arrows turn green and the ones coming in turn blue; the rest fade.",
            "- The state the simulator is running right now is filled in violet and marked \"running\", so you can watch the AI move through the graph. A state that is both running and selected keeps the violet fill with the selection border.",
            "- The box under the graph says what it takes to leave that state to each of the others (the conditions of the direct transition, or the chain of states in between when there is none, or that there is no way) and what it takes to arrive at it from each of the others.",
            "## Moving things",
            "Drag a state to move it (its place is saved in the file, only the launcher uses it). Arrange automatically puts the states in columns by the number of steps from the first state. Right drag moves the view, the wheel zooms and Fit view shows all the states."),

        new HelpTopic("Movement",
            "Movement says where the unit flies. It is in the Behavior values tab of a state, or in a behavior file. The unit always obeys its own turn rate and acceleration, so a fast turn on the map is limited by the plane it is made from.",
            "## Modes",
            "- Orbit the player: goes to a circle around the player and keeps going around it. Far away it approaches in a straight line.",
            "- Go for the player: flies straight at the player, a little ahead of where the plane is going.",
            "- Keep going straight: does not turn toward anything. It flies where it points: a pass through the player.",
            "- Wander near home: picks a random point around where the unit was born, flies there, and picks another.",
            "- Stay still: does not move. With \"Face the player\" it turns to look at the player: a turret.",
            "- Run away: flies away from the player.",
            "- Go back home: flies to the place where the unit was born and circles there.",
            "- Built-in flight: the server flies the unit with one of its own flights: chase, circle, charge, turret, route (the path of the map), kamikaze, patrol or escort. Empty means the \"behavior\" of the unit type. It is how a boss keeps its usual flight while the AI changes everything else (weapons, summons, shield). The simulator only approximates it.",
            "## Fields",
            "- Speed: a fraction of the unit's top speed (1 is the top speed). The speed multiplier of the unit type also applies.",
            "- Speed when far (orbit): speed while the player is farther than distance x far factor.",
            "- Distance: radius of the circle around the player (orbit), or of the area around home (wander).",
            "- Deviation (orbit): how many degrees ahead along the circle the unit aims. Small spirals in slowly, large goes around fast.",
            "- Far factor (orbit): \"far\" starts at distance times this value.",
            "- Lead (approach): seconds of the player's movement the unit aims ahead.",
            "- Interval (wander): seconds between two destinations.",
            "- Spin (orbit): 1 clockwise, -1 counter-clockwise, 0 random for each unit.",
            "- Built-in flight and Speed multiplier (built-in only): which flight, and how much faster or slower than the unit type says (1.2 is a fifth faster)."),

        new HelpTopic("Weapons and contact",
            "## Shoots",
            "Off means the unit does not shoot while in this state. It is how a state can be a pure flight (retreat, approach).",
            "## Weapons (mounts)",
            "The list is made from the guns of the unit in the simulator. Checked guns shoot in this state; all checked means every gun the unit has. Each gun is a section: click its name (or the arrow) to open its data under it: pattern, position, fire rate, burst, damage, speed, reach and bullet steering. Several can be open at once. At least one gun stays checked: turn Shoots off to have none.",
            "In the game this is how a boss uses different guns in different moments. The guns are the real ones of the unit, so the list changes when you choose another unit.",
            "## Fire rate multiplier",
            "Multiplies how often the guns shoot in this state (2 is twice as often).",
            "## Shooting range",
            "Distance at which the unit starts to shoot. 0 means the range of the gun itself.",
            "## Aim lead",
            "How far ahead of a moving player the unit aims (0 aims at the player). Empty uses the aim lead of the unit type.",
            "## Explodes within",
            "The unit blows up when a player gets this close: a kamikaze. In the game the explosion gives no XP and no loot. 0 means never."),

        new HelpTopic("The simulator",
            "The simulator runs a small arena with the unit that is selected, its AI, bullets and a plane that stands for the player. It uses the same code as the server for flying and shooting, so what you see is what the server will do, except for walls, rooms and other enemies (see Limits).",
            "## Controls",
            "- Play / Pause and Restart: stop the arena or start it again from the first state.",
            "- Speed: how fast the simulation runs.",
            "- Player: follows your clicks (click on the arena to send it there), flies in a circle, or stands still. Its speed and circle size are next to it.",
            "- Player in sight: off means there is no player at all, to test what the AI does when the player is gone.",
            "- Health lost %/s: the unit loses this percent of its health every second, to test health conditions.",
            "- Hit -25% and Heal: change the health of the unit at once.",
            "- Comes back after dying: the unit is born again when it dies, so the test goes on.",
            "- Speed x, Turn x, Fire x, Aim lead, Free aim: the values the unit type has in its map or enemies.json (speedMultiplier, turnMultiplier, fireRateMultiplier, aimLead, freeAim). They are loaded from the type and can be changed here to try them; this does not change the type.",
            "## What is drawn",
            "The unit and the player, their trail, the destination the AI is flying to (dashed line and cross), the bullets (each gun has its own color), the state with its time, and a log of the state changes. The HUD says whether the file is the unit's own AI or one loaded to test.",
            "Mouse: left click sends the player there, right drag moves the view and the wheel zooms."),

        new HelpTopic("Using an AI in the game",
            "An AI does nothing until a unit type uses it.",
            "- In the map editor: Units tab, pick the unit type, Enemy AI tab, choose the AI in the \"AI (file)\" field. The AI is not stored in the map: only its id is, so the same AI serves many maps.",
            "- In enemies.json (the enemies of the Nexus, tutorial and open world): write \"ai\": \"<id>\" in the type.",
            "- A boss with phases is just an AI with a state per phase (see Phases inside an AI). The Phases tab of the map editor does not exist any more.",
            "## What the server does",
            "Every enemy of that type is born with the AI and starts in its first state. The AI decides where it flies and when it shoots. The usual things the server adds afterwards still happen: enemies keep apart from each other, they stay inside the area of their room, and they cannot turn faster than their plane.",
            "The aggro radius of the old built-in behaviors is ignored: an enemy with an AI is awake when a live player exists, and the states decide when it fights.",
            "If the id does not exist the server writes a warning in its log and the unit uses its normal built-in behavior. The map check also warns about it.",
            "Typing /brains in the game chat lists the AIs the server loaded."),

        new HelpTopic("Sharing files: import and export",
            "## Export...",
            "Writes one file with the selected AI and every behavior it uses, so it can be sent to someone else in a single file.",
            "## Import...",
            "Reads such a file and adds its AIs and behaviors to your folder. Nothing of yours is overwritten: when an id already exists the new file gets another id. Before adding, it shows what the file holds.",
            "## Open folder",
            "Opens the ai folder. Files can also be copied there by hand and loaded with Reload.",
            "## Reload",
            "Reads the folder from the disk again. Files with unsaved changes are kept as they are in the launcher."),

        new HelpTopic("Menus and shortcuts",
            "## File",
            "- New AI and New behavior: ask for a name and make the file (it is saved at once, so its id exists for the maps).",
            "- Save, Save all and Revert: Save writes the file shown in the editor; Save all writes every changed file; Revert throws away the changes of the file shown and reads it from the disk. The title of a changed file in the list has a mark.",
            "- Import, Export, Open folder and Reload: see Sharing files.",
            "## Edit",
            "- Duplicate: copies the selected file with a new id.",
            "- Rename: changes the id (the file name). Unit types of enemies.json that use it keep looking for the old id until you change them, and the launcher warns before renaming.",
            "- Delete: sends the file to server\\_backup, so it is not lost for good. AIs that use a deleted behavior are left with a state that has a missing behavior.",
            "## Shortcuts",
            "- Ctrl+S: save the file in the editor.",
            "- Ctrl+Shift+S: save all.",
            "- Ctrl+N: new AI.",
            "- F1: this help.",
            "The launcher asks to save before closing when any file has changes that are not saved."),

        new HelpTopic("Limits",
            "- This is a reconstruction. The original server AI is not in any file of the game, so nothing here claims to be what the original did.",
            "- The simulator has no walls, rooms or other enemies. In the game the server also keeps enemies inside their room and apart from each other.",
            "- Bullets in the simulator are plain: no homing bullets and no gun offsets.",
            "- Summoned units are only counted in the simulator, not flown, and the built-in flights are approximated there (the server runs the real ones).",
            "- Not done yet: an undo history in the editor (use Revert), and per-state changes of the unit's health or damage.",
            "- There is no undo in this editor: use Revert to go back to the saved file."),
    };
}
